 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Holes:** Forward rotation causes holes because multiple source voxels project to the same destination voxel due to truncation. Back-rotation solves this by ensuring every destination voxel is filled [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Aliasing:** Direct inverse rotation can produce significant aliasing. Antialiasing techniques (e.g., trilinear interpolation) are recommended [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Brute force inverse rotation (computing matrix multiplication for every voxel) is computationally expensive. The incremental method reduces this by using fixed-point additions [S1].
-*   **Cache:** Storing already rotated voxels in a cache can improve performance if the contact area between the tool and object changes incrementally [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Holes:** Forward rotation causes holes because multiple source voxels project to the same destination voxel due to truncation. Back-rotation solves this by mapping destination voxels to source voxels [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Aliasing:** Direct inverse rotation produces significant aliasing. Antialiasing techniques such as trilinear interpolation are recommended [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Brute force inverse rotation has high computation time increasing with the number of voxels. The incremental method reduces this by using fixed-point additions [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Caching:** Storing already rotated voxels in a cache improves performance when the contact area between the tool and object changes incrementally [S2, unofficial].
 
